# Line endings are a fact in this repository, not a preference.
#
# `.editorconfig` already declares `end_of_line = lf`, and an editor that reads it
# will write LF. Git does not read `.editorconfig`, so on a machine with the
# Windows default of `core.autocrlf=true` a checkout rewrites every text file to
# CRLF on disk while the blob in the repository stays LF. The two agree in the
# commit history and disagree on disk, which is the worst place to disagree.
#
# It is not cosmetic. A gate that reads a source file and matches a string with a
# newline in it — the consumer gate kit's own test matches the published wording of
# a law — sees a different file on a Windows checkout than on the machine that
# wrote it, and fails on a tree that is correct. That is how the kit's
# `law-reaches-every-consumer` test came to fail on a clean checkout of a commit
# whose own test suite was green on Linux.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` is the pair that fixes both halves: git decides from content
# which files are text, and writes LF on checkout on every platform, including
# Windows. The blobs in the repository are already LF, so this changes how a
# working tree is written and not what is stored — the commit that adds this file
# touches no other file.
#
# The lockfile and the fixtures are named explicitly because they are read by the
# retired-line gate, which splits the lockfile into a graph, and a gate test that
# writes CRLF over a fixture must start from one that is not already CRLF.
/pnpm-lock.yaml text eol=lf
/scripts/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# Anything git cannot classify as text stays byte-for-byte, which is what
# `text=auto` means by itself and is why the rule above is safe to apply to the
# whole tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Generated and vendored trees are not ours to normalise.
apps/site/public/** -text
**/*.png binary
**/*.jpg binary
**/*.webp binary
**/*.woff binary
**/*.woff2 binary
